ASSEMBLY EXAMPLE - GETPID AND PRINT-INT SOURCE STUDY

A complete tested-as-source reference example lives in the repository:
  userland/examples/asm/04-print-int.s

It calls getpid (SVC number 1), converts PID 0..255 to decimal by
repeated subtraction, then writes bytes with SVC number 9.
The assembly uses bl, push/pop, cmp, bcc, strb, adr, and labels.
Because the source is longer, do not type a shortened pseudocode
version as if it were executable. Retrieve that exact file on the
host, copy its contents with MCP write_file, then run:
  as /tmp/pid.s
  /tmp/a.out
Expected: decimal PID, with no guaranteed particular value.

If you cannot access the repository source, instead run the shorter
/man/example-asm-hello example. The assembler accepts <=2048 B of
source, <=32 labels, and branch ranges are limited.
